1
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:05,300
Seven in the morning.

2
00:00:06,161 --> 00:00:07,682
You walk into the office with a cup

3
00:00:07,722 --> 00:00:08,162
of coffee.

4
00:00:08,863 --> 00:00:10,444
Your first meeting runs for two hours.

5
00:00:11,144 --> 00:00:12,185
Then come the emails,

6
00:00:12,245 --> 00:00:13,926
the reports, and one more meeting.

7
00:00:14,546 --> 00:00:16,127
The water bottle on your desk is still

8
00:00:16,247 --> 00:00:16,607
sealed.

9
00:00:17,888 --> 00:00:18,729
Three in the afternoon.

10
00:00:19,509 --> 00:00:21,310
A dull headache starts to creep in.

11
00:00:22,051 --> 00:00:23,732
You read the same line three times,

12
00:00:24,332 --> 00:00:25,553
and it still doesn't make sense.

13
00:00:26,193 --> 00:00:26,994
Your lips are dry.

14
00:00:27,014 --> 00:00:28,615
Your whole body feels sluggish.

15
00:00:29,395 --> 00:00:30,176
And at four o'clock,

16
00:00:30,776 --> 00:00:32,277
you have to stand up and present to

17
00:00:32,297 --> 00:00:32,937
the whole room.

18
00:00:33,958 --> 00:00:35,119
You reach for the water bottle,

19
00:00:35,239 --> 00:00:35,959
and then you stop.

20
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:38,060
How much is actually enough?

21
00:00:38,701 --> 00:00:40,342
Should you just force down as much as

22
00:00:40,362 --> 00:00:40,722
you can?

23
00:00:41,522 --> 00:00:43,384
Online, everyone tells you something different.

24
00:00:43,864 --> 00:00:45,786
Some say you need exactly eight glasses a

25
00:00:45,826 --> 00:00:46,006
day.

26
00:00:46,426 --> 00:00:47,447
Some say three liters.

27
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:49,328
Others say just drink when you're thirsty.

28
00:00:49,869 --> 00:00:51,370
Some even claim that the more you drink,

29
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:53,211
the more toxins you flush out.

30
00:00:53,892 --> 00:00:54,653
To find out who's right,

31
00:00:54,953 --> 00:00:56,834
let's first look at what water actually does

32
00:00:56,954 --> 00:00:57,655
inside your body.

33
00:00:58,315 --> 00:00:59,935
If you poured your body into a jar,

34
00:01:00,515 --> 00:01:02,075
most of what filled it would be water.

35
00:01:02,916 --> 00:01:05,396
In an adult, water makes up about sixty

36
00:01:05,436 --> 00:01:06,536
percent of body weight.

37
00:01:07,116 --> 00:01:08,336
Newborn babies have even more,

38
00:01:08,756 --> 00:01:10,156
about seventy-five percent.

39
00:01:10,936 --> 00:01:12,037
Older adults have less,

40
00:01:12,657 --> 00:01:13,917
around fifty-five percent.

41
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:16,477
And every organ holds a different amount.

42
00:01:17,257 --> 00:01:19,958
According to a classic analysis from 1945,

43
00:01:20,438 --> 00:01:22,318
the brain and heart are about seventy-three

44
00:01:22,378 --> 00:01:23,118
percent water.

45
00:01:23,938 --> 00:01:26,018
The lungs are about eighty-three percent.

46
00:01:26,358 --> 00:01:28,459
Bones, only about thirty-one percent.

47
00:01:29,099 --> 00:01:31,179
About two-thirds of all that water sits inside

48
00:01:31,219 --> 00:01:31,719
your cells.

49
00:01:32,599 --> 00:01:33,579
The rest is outside:

50
00:01:34,159 --> 00:01:36,419
in your blood, and in the spaces between

51
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:36,799
cells.

52
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:39,100
And this water never sits still.

53
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:41,480
It flows, it evaporates,

54
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,280
it gets filtered, and it is replaced every

55
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:44,860
single day.

56
00:01:45,701 --> 00:01:47,841
To see why, let's follow a drop of

57
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:49,641
water through its three main jobs.

58
00:01:50,341 --> 00:01:53,362
Job number one: water is your delivery system.

59
00:01:53,902 --> 00:01:55,062
The liquid part of your blood,

60
00:01:55,102 --> 00:01:57,163
called plasma, is mostly water.

61
00:01:57,923 --> 00:02:00,064
That stream carries nutrients and oxygen to

62
00:02:00,264 --> 00:02:00,844
every cell.

63
00:02:01,624 --> 00:02:02,185
On the way back,

64
00:02:02,565 --> 00:02:03,985
it picks up the waste that needs to

65
00:02:04,025 --> 00:02:04,225
go.

66
00:02:04,945 --> 00:02:07,386
In your gut, your body releases around eight

67
00:02:07,446 --> 00:02:10,227
liters of digestive fluids every day to process

68
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:10,527
food.

69
00:02:11,407 --> 00:02:13,188
Then almost all of that water is absorbed

70
00:02:13,248 --> 00:02:13,728
back again.

71
00:02:14,388 --> 00:02:15,508
When you run low on water,

72
00:02:15,829 --> 00:02:17,249
your blood volume drops a little.

73
00:02:17,749 --> 00:02:19,390
And your heart has to work harder to

74
00:02:19,430 --> 00:02:20,810
keep the flow from slowing down.

75
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:24,171
Job number two: water keeps you from overheating.

76
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:26,131
When your body temperature rises,

77
00:02:26,191 --> 00:02:27,652
your skin releases sweat.

78
00:02:28,352 --> 00:02:29,412
As the sweat evaporates,

79
00:02:29,772 --> 00:02:31,232
it carries heat away from your body.

80
00:02:31,753 --> 00:02:33,173
It's like standing in front of a fan

81
00:02:33,213 --> 00:02:34,493
with a wet towel on your face.

82
00:02:35,253 --> 00:02:36,014
The hotter it gets,

83
00:02:36,434 --> 00:02:37,294
and the more you move,

84
00:02:37,654 --> 00:02:38,374
the more you sweat.

85
00:02:39,114 --> 00:02:40,595
During hard exercise in the sun,

86
00:02:40,935 --> 00:02:42,915
you can lose around two liters of sweat

87
00:02:42,955 --> 00:02:43,355
an hour.

88
00:02:44,016 --> 00:02:45,456
And every drop of that has to be

89
00:02:45,496 --> 00:02:45,916
replaced.

90
00:02:46,656 --> 00:02:49,258
Job number three: water helps your kidneys

91
00:02:49,298 --> 00:02:49,919
clean your blood.

92
00:02:50,719 --> 00:02:53,021
Every day, your two kidneys filter about one

93
00:02:53,141 --> 00:02:55,143
hundred and eighty liters of fluid from your

94
00:02:55,183 --> 00:02:55,423
blood.

95
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:57,404
That's almost a full bathtub.

96
00:02:58,065 --> 00:02:59,806
But more than ninety-nine percent of it is

97
00:02:59,866 --> 00:03:00,627
absorbed back.

98
00:03:01,347 --> 00:03:03,069
Only about one and a half liters becomes

99
00:03:03,209 --> 00:03:05,471
urine, carrying waste out of your body.

100
00:03:06,311 --> 00:03:07,552
Your kidneys can also adjust:

101
00:03:07,952 --> 00:03:09,374
hold on to water when you're short,

102
00:03:09,874 --> 00:03:11,195
let more go when you have extra.

103
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:13,617
But this filter has a speed limit.

104
00:03:14,337 --> 00:03:15,158
Remember that detail.

105
00:03:15,618 --> 00:03:16,459
It will matter later.

106
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:19,701
Now, let's put everything on a scale.

107
00:03:20,441 --> 00:03:22,041
On the left is the water you lose:

108
00:03:22,562 --> 00:03:24,182
through urine, through sweat,

109
00:03:24,522 --> 00:03:25,583
and with every breath.

110
00:03:26,523 --> 00:03:28,504
Even sitting still in an air-conditioned room,

111
00:03:28,904 --> 00:03:29,984
you're still losing water.

112
00:03:30,704 --> 00:03:31,945
On the right is the water you take

113
00:03:32,005 --> 00:03:34,306
in: from drinks, and from food too.

114
00:03:35,066 --> 00:03:36,547
Your body even makes a little water when

115
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:38,947
it burns food, but that part is very

116
00:03:39,027 --> 00:03:39,328
small.

117
00:03:40,188 --> 00:03:42,749
For adults, the daily need is around two

118
00:03:42,849 --> 00:03:44,709
to three liters, food included.

119
00:03:45,610 --> 00:03:47,050
As soon as the two sides fall out

120
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:48,911
of balance, your body reacts.

121
00:03:49,651 --> 00:03:51,011
Because deep inside your brain,

122
00:03:51,051 --> 00:03:52,012
there's a sensor.

123
00:03:52,532 --> 00:03:54,392
It constantly checks whether your blood is

124
00:03:54,472 --> 00:03:55,973
diluted or concentrated.

125
00:03:56,653 --> 00:03:58,373
It works like the thermostat on an air

126
00:03:58,393 --> 00:04:00,934
conditioner, except it measures water instead

127
00:04:00,954 --> 00:04:01,494
of temperature.

128
00:04:02,214 --> 00:04:03,195
When you're short on water,

129
00:04:03,235 --> 00:04:04,755
your blood gets more concentrated.

130
00:04:05,295 --> 00:04:06,916
Just one to two percent more,

131
00:04:07,316 --> 00:04:09,156
and the sensor switches on a warning light.

132
00:04:09,936 --> 00:04:12,077
First, your brain releases a hormone called

133
00:04:12,097 --> 00:04:12,877
ADH.

134
00:04:13,457 --> 00:04:15,378
ADH tells your kidneys to hold on to

135
00:04:15,418 --> 00:04:15,698
water.

136
00:04:16,358 --> 00:04:18,138
You pee less, and it gets darker.

137
00:04:19,039 --> 00:04:19,779
If that's not enough,

138
00:04:20,159 --> 00:04:21,699
your brain sends a second signal:

139
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:22,460
thirst.

140
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,840
You drink, your blood is diluted again,

141
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,501
and the warning light goes off.

142
00:04:27,681 --> 00:04:30,202
In healthy people, this system runs quietly,

143
00:04:30,422 --> 00:04:31,382
day and night.

144
00:04:31,882 --> 00:04:32,902
But in older adults,

145
00:04:33,002 --> 00:04:34,323
thirst often arrives later,

146
00:04:34,843 --> 00:04:35,363
and weaker.

147
00:04:35,943 --> 00:04:37,784
So what happens when you ignore these signals?

148
00:04:38,404 --> 00:04:40,244
In one experiment, healthy young women were

149
00:04:40,284 --> 00:04:41,705
made mildly dehydrated.

150
00:04:42,265 --> 00:04:43,885
They lost about one point four percent of

151
00:04:43,905 --> 00:04:44,525
their body weight.

152
00:04:45,126 --> 00:04:46,746
They got headaches, felt tired,

153
00:04:47,206 --> 00:04:48,227
found it harder to focus,

154
00:04:48,287 --> 00:04:50,107
and everything felt like more effort.

155
00:04:50,887 --> 00:04:52,148
Another experiment, in men,

156
00:04:52,588 --> 00:04:53,568
found they were less alert.

157
00:04:54,108 --> 00:04:55,749
Their working memory dipped as well.

158
00:04:56,489 --> 00:04:58,450
The results across studies don't fully agree

159
00:04:58,470 --> 00:04:58,650
yet.

160
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:01,570
But for exercise, the picture is clearer.

161
00:05:02,211 --> 00:05:03,731
Losing more than two percent of your body

162
00:05:03,751 --> 00:05:06,272
weight to dehydration can reduce your endurance.

163
00:05:06,892 --> 00:05:07,852
Especially in the heat.

164
00:05:08,732 --> 00:05:10,973
Severe dehydration, like from long bouts of

165
00:05:11,053 --> 00:05:13,554
diarrhea or vomiting, can be dangerous.

166
00:05:14,334 --> 00:05:16,015
Then you need to replace both water and

167
00:05:16,095 --> 00:05:18,835
salts, with an oral rehydration solution,

168
00:05:19,356 --> 00:05:20,856
and see a doctor right away.

169
00:05:21,876 --> 00:05:22,216
Headache.

170
00:05:22,777 --> 00:05:23,197
Sluggish.

171
00:05:23,757 --> 00:05:24,437
Hard to focus.

172
00:05:24,997 --> 00:05:25,577
Sound familiar?

173
00:05:26,157 --> 00:05:27,938
That's you, at three in the afternoon.

174
00:05:28,558 --> 00:05:29,999
So, on a normal day,

175
00:05:30,079 --> 00:05:30,780
how should you drink?

176
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:34,122
A: exactly eight glasses a day,

177
00:05:34,322 --> 00:05:35,703
no more, no less.

178
00:05:36,844 --> 00:05:38,686
B: drink when you're thirsty,

179
00:05:39,106 --> 00:05:40,167
drink more when it's hot,

180
00:05:40,467 --> 00:05:42,008
when you exercise or when you're sick,

181
00:05:42,528 --> 00:05:43,589
and keep an eye on the color of

182
00:05:43,609 --> 00:05:44,010
your pee.

183
00:05:44,950 --> 00:05:46,411
Or C: the more,

184
00:05:46,431 --> 00:05:46,732
the better?

185
00:05:47,933 --> 00:05:49,178
Pause the video and take a guess.

186
00:05:58,412 --> 00:05:59,734
The key is the scale.

187
00:06:00,515 --> 00:06:02,018
Your body doesn't need a fixed number.

188
00:06:02,318 --> 00:06:05,543
It needs balance: water in equals water out.

189
00:06:06,104 --> 00:06:07,767
And it already has a built-in sensor to

190
00:06:07,807 --> 00:06:08,588
keep that balance.

191
00:06:09,469 --> 00:06:11,032
So where did the eight-glasses number come

192
00:06:11,072 --> 00:06:11,232
from?

193
00:06:12,333 --> 00:06:14,193
Eight glasses, at about two hundred and fifty

194
00:06:14,233 --> 00:06:16,794
milliliters each, comes to about two liters.

195
00:06:17,894 --> 00:06:20,414
In 1945, a nutrition board in the United

196
00:06:20,454 --> 00:06:21,754
States made a recommendation.

197
00:06:22,614 --> 00:06:24,295
Each person needs about two and a half

198
00:06:24,355 --> 00:06:25,415
liters of water a day.

199
00:06:26,255 --> 00:06:27,535
But in the very next sentence,

200
00:06:27,875 --> 00:06:29,575
they wrote that most of this amount is

201
00:06:29,755 --> 00:06:30,755
already in your food.

202
00:06:31,695 --> 00:06:35,096
In 2002, the physiologist Heinz Valtin traced

203
00:06:35,136 --> 00:06:37,116
the origin of the eight-glasses advice.

204
00:06:37,836 --> 00:06:39,576
He suggested that the second sentence had

205
00:06:39,637 --> 00:06:40,917
simply been forgotten.

206
00:06:41,617 --> 00:06:43,737
And he found no scientific evidence that everyone

207
00:06:43,797 --> 00:06:45,937
must drink exactly eight glasses.

208
00:06:46,777 --> 00:06:49,098
Today, the European Food Safety Authority

209
00:06:49,138 --> 00:06:50,218
gives a reference value.

210
00:06:50,738 --> 00:06:52,418
About two liters a day for women.

211
00:06:53,218 --> 00:06:54,978
For men, about two and a half liters.

212
00:06:55,879 --> 00:06:58,159
That's total water, including the water in

213
00:06:58,239 --> 00:07:00,159
food, under normal conditions.

214
00:07:00,939 --> 00:07:01,719
In the United States,

215
00:07:01,759 --> 00:07:03,019
the reference values are higher.

216
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,340
About two point seven liters for women,

217
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:07,320
and three point seven liters for men.

218
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:10,220
The numbers differ because each is based on

219
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:10,940
different data.

220
00:07:11,581 --> 00:07:13,281
They reflect how much water healthy people

221
00:07:13,321 --> 00:07:14,801
there typically take in each day.

222
00:07:15,521 --> 00:07:16,842
So where does your water come from?

223
00:07:17,642 --> 00:07:19,443
About a fifth to a third comes from

224
00:07:19,543 --> 00:07:22,124
food: vegetables, fruit, soup,

225
00:07:22,344 --> 00:07:22,645
rice.

226
00:07:23,545 --> 00:07:25,946
Watermelon, for example, is more than ninety

227
00:07:25,986 --> 00:07:26,707
percent water.

228
00:07:27,127 --> 00:07:28,307
Tea and coffee count too.

229
00:07:28,988 --> 00:07:30,929
A study in 2014 followed men who were

230
00:07:30,989 --> 00:07:32,009
regular coffee drinkers.

231
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:33,710
At four cups a day,

232
00:07:33,790 --> 00:07:36,191
coffee hydrated them about as well as plain

233
00:07:36,231 --> 00:07:36,511
water.

234
00:07:37,272 --> 00:07:39,053
Soft drinks and juice contain water too,

235
00:07:39,673 --> 00:07:40,853
but they come with a lot of sugar

236
00:07:40,893 --> 00:07:41,474
and calories.

237
00:07:42,114 --> 00:07:43,895
So plain water is still the best choice

238
00:07:43,915 --> 00:07:44,735
for everyday thirst.

239
00:07:45,356 --> 00:07:46,876
And to check whether you're drinking enough,

240
00:07:47,217 --> 00:07:48,137
there's a simple trick:

241
00:07:48,937 --> 00:07:50,278
look at the color of your pee.

242
00:07:51,299 --> 00:07:53,600
Pale straw yellow usually means you're well

243
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:54,100
hydrated.

244
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,301
Dark yellow, like strong tea,

245
00:07:56,781 --> 00:07:58,362
is a sign you should drink more.

246
00:07:59,282 --> 00:08:01,043
Some medicines and vitamins can change the

247
00:08:01,083 --> 00:08:03,324
color too, so treat this as a hint,

248
00:08:03,845 --> 00:08:04,405
not a test.

249
00:08:05,045 --> 00:08:06,285
There are times when you need to drink

250
00:08:06,325 --> 00:08:07,025
more on purpose.

251
00:08:07,606 --> 00:08:08,126
When it's hot.

252
00:08:08,486 --> 00:08:09,506
When you exercise hard.

253
00:08:09,966 --> 00:08:10,826
When you have a fever,

254
00:08:10,966 --> 00:08:12,107
vomiting or diarrhea.

255
00:08:12,887 --> 00:08:14,707
Pregnant and breastfeeding women need extra

256
00:08:14,747 --> 00:08:15,287
water too.

257
00:08:16,147 --> 00:08:17,828
Older adults should drink regularly,

258
00:08:18,148 --> 00:08:19,488
because thirst may come late.

259
00:08:20,108 --> 00:08:20,788
But on the other hand,

260
00:08:21,028 --> 00:08:22,249
more is not always better.

261
00:08:22,749 --> 00:08:24,449
Remember the filter with a speed limit?

262
00:08:25,289 --> 00:08:26,230
If you drink too much,

263
00:08:26,390 --> 00:08:29,090
too fast, the extra water dilutes the sodium

264
00:08:29,110 --> 00:08:29,610
in your blood.

265
00:08:30,190 --> 00:08:31,971
This is called hyponatremia,

266
00:08:32,371 --> 00:08:33,211
and it can be dangerous.

267
00:08:33,871 --> 00:08:35,612
At the 2002 Boston Marathon,

268
00:08:35,632 --> 00:08:37,112
about thirteen percent of the runners who

269
00:08:37,132 --> 00:08:39,192
were tested had hyponatremia.

270
00:08:40,033 --> 00:08:41,573
The risk was highest in those who gained

271
00:08:41,613 --> 00:08:42,413
weight during the race.

272
00:08:43,173 --> 00:08:45,374
In other words, they drank more than they

273
00:08:45,454 --> 00:08:46,034
sweated out.

274
00:08:46,874 --> 00:08:48,374
And if you have heart or kidney disease,

275
00:08:48,875 --> 00:08:50,795
follow your doctor's advice on how much to

276
00:08:50,835 --> 00:08:51,095
drink.

277
00:08:51,795 --> 00:08:53,456
Now let's rule out the options one by

278
00:08:53,577 --> 00:08:53,657
one.

279
00:08:54,497 --> 00:08:57,119
Option A: a fixed eight glasses.

280
00:08:58,080 --> 00:08:59,781
There's no solid basis for this number,

281
00:09:00,222 --> 00:09:01,803
and everyone's needs are different.

282
00:09:02,704 --> 00:09:02,904
Out.

283
00:09:04,185 --> 00:09:05,686
Option C: the more,

284
00:09:05,846 --> 00:09:06,387
the better.

285
00:09:06,827 --> 00:09:08,989
Drinking too much can lead to hyponatremia.

286
00:09:09,729 --> 00:09:09,910
Out.

287
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:11,871
That leaves option B:

288
00:09:12,532 --> 00:09:13,533
listen to your thirst,

289
00:09:14,233 --> 00:09:15,314
drink more when you need to,

290
00:09:15,994 --> 00:09:17,856
and watch the color of your pee.

291
00:09:18,496 --> 00:09:19,537
The answer is B.

292
00:09:20,258 --> 00:09:21,500
Back to three in the afternoon.

293
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:23,322
You take a few sips of water,

294
00:09:23,682 --> 00:09:25,725
then one more glass before your presentation.

295
00:09:26,505 --> 00:09:28,187
No need to force down a whole liter

296
00:09:28,227 --> 00:09:28,608
at once.

297
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:31,431
From tomorrow, you keep the bottle where you

298
00:09:31,471 --> 00:09:33,313
can see it, and drink whenever you feel

299
00:09:33,373 --> 00:09:33,774
thirsty.

300
00:09:34,695 --> 00:09:35,436
A few hours later,

301
00:09:35,876 --> 00:09:37,699
your pee is back to a pale straw

302
00:09:37,759 --> 00:09:38,079
yellow.

303
00:09:38,679 --> 00:09:40,340
And there's one more surprising benefit.

304
00:09:41,361 --> 00:09:44,123
In 1996, Italian scientists followed nearly

305
00:09:44,243 --> 00:09:46,564
two hundred people who had had kidney stones,

306
00:09:46,905 --> 00:09:47,945
for five years.

307
00:09:48,926 --> 00:09:50,567
One group was told to drink enough water

308
00:09:50,607 --> 00:09:52,028
to pee at least two liters a day.

309
00:09:52,788 --> 00:09:54,490
After five years, only about twelve percent

310
00:09:54,510 --> 00:09:55,770
of that group had stones come back.

311
00:09:56,271 --> 00:09:56,931
In the other group,

312
00:09:57,111 --> 00:09:59,033
the number was twenty-seven percent.

313
00:09:59,833 --> 00:10:02,375
The American Urological Association's 2014

314
00:10:02,415 --> 00:10:04,897
guideline advises people who've had stones

315
00:10:04,957 --> 00:10:05,797
to drink enough.

316
00:10:06,598 --> 00:10:07,798
Enough to pee at least two and a

317
00:10:07,838 --> 00:10:08,559
half liters a day.

318
00:10:09,259 --> 00:10:10,000
Four in the afternoon.

319
00:10:10,701 --> 00:10:12,022
Your presentation goes smoothly,

320
00:10:12,243 --> 00:10:13,604
and the whole room applauds.

321
00:10:14,125 --> 00:10:15,406
You smile, take a bow,

322
00:10:15,747 --> 00:10:16,568
and thank everyone.

323
00:10:17,329 --> 00:10:19,031
Then you dash straight for the bathroom.

324
00:10:19,691 --> 00:10:22,054
Your body, clearly, is keeping its balance

325
00:10:22,154 --> 00:10:22,735
just fine.
